====== Replace Separate Replication Tasks with One Recursive Task ======

This procedure brings every dataset to the same migration snapshot, then switches to one recursive replication task. Datasets that are already replicated are sent incrementally from their existing shared snapshot. Datasets that have **no** backup yet are sent in full. A full resend of already-replicated data is not forced.

The selected root may be a pool's root dataset or a dataset below it. This replicates that dataset and its children, not the pool's configuration.

**You use one Python script, four times.** It asks for the customer's settings once and remembers them. No Bash variables or separate ZFS commands are needed.

==== Which backup root to use ====

A recursive task needs the source root **and** every child to share a snapshot with the backup. Per-dataset tasks never replicate the source root itself, so the backup root usually has no shared snapshot (e.g. ''%%main -> backup%%'' where ''%%backup%%'' is the backup pool). That cannot be fixed in place without overwriting the backup root.

The script therefore supports two modes, chosen by the backup roots you enter:

^ Mode ^ When ^ What happens ^
| **Relocate** (typical) | New backup root differs from the existing one, e.g. existing ''%%backup%%'', new ''%%backup/main%%'' | The new root is created by a full send of the source root. Each usable existing replica is **renamed** under it on the backup (same pool, snapshots kept) and then sent incrementally. Datasets with no usable replica are sent in full. |
| **In place** | New backup root left blank (same as existing) | Only works if the backup root already shares a snapshot with the source root. Missing children are sent in full. |

If ''%%check%%'' reports ''%%Backup copy has no snapshots. Re-run check with a NEW backup root%%'' for the root, use relocate mode.

Existing replicas are matched by relative path: source ''%%main/a/b%%'' is matched to ''%%<existing root>/a/b%%''. A replica stored anywhere else is not found, and that dataset is sent in full. Service must check the readiness report for unexpected ''%%FULL%%'' rows.

===== Before You Start =====

Service must first approve the source root, existing and new backup roots, SSH settings, ZFS compatibility, available backup space, and backup state. Keep a recoverable backup and record the old task settings.

**This procedure is for unencrypted, non-clone datasets.** Encryption and clones need a separate plan. Never force a full transfer into a populated backup dataset or pool root; the script refuses to do so.

Full sends of datasets without a backup copy all of their data. Service must confirm network time and backup capacity for those datasets from the transfer report.

All sends use ''%%-L -e -c%%'' (large blocks, embedded data, compressed). This matches replicas originally sent with or without large blocks. The backup pool must have the ''%%large_blocks%%'' and ''%%embedded_data%%'' features enabled (''%%zpool get feature@large_blocks,feature@embedded_data <pool>%%'').

The script checks and copies datasets one at a time over a single reused SSH connection. With hundreds of datasets, ''%%check%%'', ''%%prepare%%'' and ''%%verify%%'' can take several minutes before any data moves. Run the commands inside ''%%tmux%%'' or ''%%screen%%'' so a dropped terminal does not interrupt ''%%copy%%''.

Service must test this script on a small representative hierarchy and confirm the installed replication software can use manually created migration snapshots. The script does not verify those software capabilities or available capacity.

**At any error: stop and give Service the reports and log. Do not enable force/overwrite, delete snapshots, or try to fix a partial receive yourself.**

**Backup permissions:** the backup must accept ''%%zfs receive -s%%'' (resumable receive) and ''%%zfs rename%%'' over SSH for the chosen account.

===== 1. Pause the Old Tasks =====

Let active transfers finish. Disable the old replication schedules and snapshot/pruning automation affecting this hierarchy on either server.

**Keep the tasks and snapshots.** Until handover is complete, do not write to the backup or create, rename, or delete datasets in either selected hierarchy. Keep pruning paused.

Source applications may continue writing. If application-consistent backups are required, arrange an application pause for Step 4. A recursive ZFS snapshot alone does not guarantee application consistency.

===== 2. Download the Script to the Source Server =====

Download this custom helper:

{{:zfs-replication-consolidate.py|Download zfs-replication-consolidate.py}}

Place it in an agreed working directory on the **source server**. It is supplied with this procedure, not installed by ZFS or Cockpit. No copy is needed on the backup.

Open a terminal in that directory. Run every command below there, using the same account. You may close and reopen the terminal between steps; settings are stored in a file, not in shell variables.

Requirements: Python 3.8 or newer, local ZFS permissions, and SSH access with non-interactive backup-side ZFS permissions. SSH authentication and the backup's verified host key must already be configured. Do not disable host-key checking to bypass an error.

===== 3. Check That the Datasets Are Ready =====

<code bash>
python3 zfs-replication-consolidate.py check
</code>

Enter these values when prompted:

^ Prompt ^ Example ^
| Source dataset root | ''%%main%%'' |
| Backup root that holds the EXISTING replicas | ''%%backup%%'' |
| Backup root for the NEW recursive task | ''%%backup/main%%'' (must not exist yet), or blank for in-place mode |
| Backup SSH destination | ''%%root@backup-host%%'' |

A blank port uses 22; a blank identity uses SSH defaults. Never enter an SSH password or private-key contents into these settings prompts.

This checks both hierarchies, including the root and parents. It compares snapshot GUIDs and checks for conflicting backup state, encryption, clones, and unfinished receives. **It creates no ZFS snapshots, holds, renames, or transfers.**

Each dataset in the readiness report is labelled:

  * ''%%[INCREMENTAL]%%'': existing replica, sent incrementally.
  * ''%%[MOVE + INCREMENTAL]%%'': existing replica, renamed under the new root on the backup, then sent incrementally. Its children move with it.
  * ''%%[FULL]%%'': no usable replica; full send. A note explains any existing, unusable copy, which is left in place.

''%%Left in place%%'' lists backup datasets that are not part of the new task (for example, the old backup root and unusable copies). Nothing is deleted.

**Continue only after ''%%CHECK PASSED%%'' and Service approval of the readiness report (''%%migration-state.readiness-<time>.txt%%'').** If the report contains blockers, stop. A blocked check saves no state, so after Service review you can run ''%%check%%'' again with corrected answers. The migration snapshot name must be unused on both sides.

The script creates ''%%migration-state.json%%'' to remember the settings and plan. Keep this file; do not edit or delete it.

===== 4. Prepare the Migration Snapshots =====

If application consistency is required, pause the applications now. Then run:

<code bash>
python3 zfs-replication-consolidate.py prepare
</code>

After Service approves the readiness report, type ''%%PREPARE%%'' at the confirmation prompt.

The script rechecks the plan, protects the incremental starting snapshots on both servers, and creates one recursive ''%%@migration-to-recursive%%'' snapshot set on the source. It protects those snapshots and writes transfer estimates.

**Continue only after ''%%PREPARE PASSED%%''.** Applications paused only for snapshot creation may then resume. Keep pruning paused and the dataset layout fixed.

Service must review the transfer report (''%%migration-state.transfer-<time>.txt%%'') and approve the mappings, renames, estimates, and backup capacity. Stream-size estimates are not a guarantee of final disk-space usage.

===== 5. Copy the Migration Snapshots =====

<code bash>
python3 zfs-replication-consolidate.py copy
</code>

After Service approves the transfer report, type ''%%COPY%%'' at the confirmation prompt.

The script processes datasets parent-first, one at a time. For each it rechecks the approved state, then:

  * creates the new root or a missing dataset by a full send,
  * renames an existing replica under the new root (relocate mode), then sends it incrementally,
  * verifies the received snapshot and protects it on the backup.

All receives use ''%%-u%%'' (not mounted) and ''%%-s%%'' (resumable); there is no forced rollback. Completed transfers are reported as ''%%VERIFIED%%''; already-complete datasets on a restart are ''%%SKIPPED%%''.

**Renamed replicas change mount path on the backup** if their mountpoint is inherited (e.g. ''%%/backup/data%%'' becomes ''%%/backup/main/data%%''). Anything on the backup server that uses the old path must be updated. The new root itself is not mounted.

**Continue only after ''%%COPY PASSED%%''.** Keep ''%%migration-state.log.jsonl%%'' with the reports and state file.

If the command stops, give Service the error and log. After Service approves, run the same ''%%copy%%'' command again: it skips completed datasets and **resumes** an interrupted receive using its resume token (after checking the token targets the expected migration snapshot). Do not restart ''%%check%%'' or ''%%prepare%%''.

===== 6. Verify Before Handover =====

<code bash>
python3 zfs-replication-consolidate.py verify
</code>

**Continue only after ''%%VERIFY PASSED%%''.** Every mapped dataset under the new backup root, including the root and parents, must have matching migration snapshot GUIDs, no pending receives, and the required holds. No unexpected datasets may exist under the new backup root.

The script checks referenced data written since the latest backup snapshot; this is not a complete change audit. Service must also confirm no other process modified the backup. Preventing receive-time mounting does not unmount an already mounted backup or make it read-only.

===== 7. Test the New Task =====

Create one replication task using the approved source root and the **new** backup root (the one entered for the new recursive task, e.g. ''%%backup/main%%''):

  * Enable **Send Recursive**.
  * Enable **Send Compressed**. The migration snapshots were sent with large blocks (''%%-L%%''); a later incremental without it can be refused by the backup.
  * Leave the schedule disabled.
  * Use the approved connection, transfer, and retention settings.
  * Keep **Force Full Resync**, **Allow Overwrite** and **Auto-Recover** **off**.

Run **Dry Run**. The scheduler accepts any snapshot shared by both sides as the starting point, even one it did not create. The log should show:

<code>
Incremental base is shared with the destination but not owned by this task.
Most recent common snapshot: <source root>@migration-to-recursive
</code>

The scheduler's retention does not prune ''%%@migration-to-recursive%%'' because it does not carry the task's tag or name. If the task sends properties (recursive sends use ''%%zfs send -R%%''), also confirm that no source ''%%mountpoint%%'' will mount over paths on the backup server.

**Stop if it proposes a full resend, selects an unexpected starting point, or requests rollback/overwrite.** Do not enable those options to bypass the problem.

Then click **Run Now**. Confirm a successful real transfer and matching new snapshot GUIDs throughout the hierarchy. Dry Run alone does not prove the backup will accept the transfer.

Use the task's logs and new snapshots for this handover check. The script's ''%%verify%%'' mode is a pre-handover check: it expects the migration snapshot to remain the latest backup snapshot, so it is not suitable after the new task advances the backup.

===== 8. Enable the New Schedule =====

After the real test succeeds and Service approves, enable the new schedule. Keep the old tasks disabled until the customer accepts the handover. **Never run both task sets against the same backup hierarchy.**

Service must review snapshot/pruning automation before re-enabling it. Keep the migration records and holds until a newer shared recursive snapshot is verified.

Cleanup is a separate approved operation. The script does not delete snapshots, datasets, or holds. Its unique hold tag is recorded in the state file and reports. Service can release those specific holds, review old snapshots, and review the ''%%Left in place%%'' datasets after handover; never remove the last usable shared snapshot or holds belonging to another workflow.

===== If Anything Goes Wrong =====

  * **Script missing:** return to Step 2. This is a supplied helper, not a system command.
  * **Check blocked:** give Service the readiness report. Do not prepare or copy. After review, run ''%%check%%'' again (for example in relocate mode).
  * **''%%Backup has data written after its latest snapshot%%'':** something changed that backup replica (often a mounted backup with ''%%atime=on%%'', or a user writing to it). An incremental receive will be refused. Service decides whether those backup-side changes can be discarded (''%%zfs rollback%%'' to the latest snapshot) and then reruns ''%%check%%''. In relocate mode, an unusable replica is sent in full instead and the old copy is left in place.
  * **Prepare failed:** stop. Snapshots or holds may already exist; do not repeat preparation or delete the state file.
  * **Copy interrupted:** after Service review, run ''%%copy%%'' again; it resumes the interrupted receive.
  * **State file already exists:** keep it. Use it to continue the existing migration; do not start over without Service review.
  * **Migration abandoned:** Service must inspect the backup history and partial receives. In relocate mode, the log lists every ''%%RENAMED%%'' replica; Service must rename those back (''%%zfs rename <new> <old>%%'') before re-enabling the old tasks.

For a new Service-approved attempt after a **passed** check or a started migration, use a different state filename with ''%%--state%%'' on **every** command. Keep the previous reports. For example, ''%%python3 zfs-replication-consolidate.py check --state reviewed-attempt.json%%'' starts a separate readiness check; it is not a way to bypass existing migration snapshots or partial receives.

===== References =====

Use the installed ZFS version's manuals when reviewing the customer's transfer plan.

